n-cp-in-gecode/","score":3,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"mzl","user_is_author":true,"tags":["programming"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/rwlg3h","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/rwlg3h/planning_with_open_variable_lists_gecode"},{"short_id":"ihvhfk","created_at":"2026-11-03T10:14:27.370-06:00","title":"A Second Futamura Projection","url":"https://blog.veitheller.de/A_Second_Futamura_Projection.html","score":5,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"abhin4v","user_is_author":false,"tags":["compilers","plt"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ihvhfk","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ihvhfk/second_futamura_projection"},{"short_id":"wpahfd","created_at":"2026-11-03T01:33:25.774-06:00","title":"Why we're moving our warehouse off DuckDB","url":"https://posthog.com/blog/moving-off-duckdb","score":7,"flags":0,"comment_count":9,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"arcatan","user_is_author":false,"tags":["databases"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/wpahfd","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/wpahfd/why_we_re_moving_our_warehouse_off_duckdb"},{"short_id":"gwyv50","created_at":"2026-11-03T11:43:09.945-06:00","title":"kitty 0.50.0","url":"https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/changelog/#id1","score":4,"flags":1,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"fuzzypixelz","user_is_author":false,"tags":["release","unix"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/gwyv50","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/gwyv50/kitty_0_50_0"},{"short_id":"r3gton","created_at":"2026-11-03T12:54:55.545-06:00","title":"Your terminal manipulation/navigation setups","url":"","score":2,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"\u003cp\u003eDear Lobste.rs, over a year after \u003ca href=\"https://lobste.rs/s/o0x7rb/your_mouse_free_setups\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003emy previous post on mouse free setups\u003c/a\u003e, your advice was instrumental to building the tech stack I use today as a disabled person, composed of the Svalboard, Talon Voice, and a fair few extensions for Talon (including ones I've written myself.)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOne aspect of my work and personal life that has remained stubbornly able-bodied has been, surprisingly, my terminal use. Reading, manipulating, and editing the terminal has required keyboard and mouse use at every step:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWhen reading the terminal, scrolling is a must, which demands the use of a physical scrolling device, one of my most consistently difficult actions.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWhen writing a terminal command, things can range from easy to brutal. Talon has a lot of built in terminal commands available, but composing complex commands still requires a great deal of typing. The most irritating aspect to me is editing a previous command, which consists of either holding (Ctrl+) an arrow key and waiting until I hit the right character, often overshooting, and then having to go back the opposite direction with the arrow keys again, OR moving with my mouse and clicking on the correct position.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWhen manipulating the terminal, this usually winds up boiling down to copy and pasting text from the output. This is universally a fine grained mouse movement to highlight and copy the correct text, often to paste it into the same terminal.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eToo often I find myself combining the second and third actions, which means a lot of arrow key or mouse movement to get to the position in my command, mouse movement to copy and paste text, and then rinsing and repeating a handful of times.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI am fairly convinced there is no true voice first terminal experience-- this is something I am hoping to change. In order to accomplish this and maybe make my life easier, I want to know the landscape of how various people interact with their terminals in 2026. I feel like my approach is deeply inefficient and must be missing something obvious...? We've had terminals since before mice existed, surely there are better ways.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"Dear Lobste.rs, over a year after [my previous post on mouse free setups](https://lobste.rs/s/o0x7rb/your_mouse_free_setups), your advice was instrumental to building the tech stack I use today as a disabled person, composed of the Svalboard, Talon Voice, and a fair few extensions for Talon (including ones I've written myself.)\r\n\r\nOne aspect of my work and personal life that has remained stubbornly able-bodied has been, surprisingly, my terminal use. Reading, manipulating, and editing the terminal has required keyboard and mouse use at every step:\r\n\r\n- When reading the terminal, scrolling is a must, which demands the use of a physical scrolling device, one of my most consistently difficult actions.\r\n- When writing a terminal command, things can range from easy to brutal. Talon has a lot of built in terminal commands available, but composing complex commands still requires a great deal of typing. The most irritating aspect to me is editing a previous command, which consists of either holding (Ctrl+) an arrow key and waiting until I hit the right character, often overshooting, and then having to go back the opposite direction with the arrow keys again, OR moving with my mouse and clicking on the correct position.\r\n- When manipulating the terminal, this usually winds up boiling down to copy and pasting text from the output. This is universally a fine grained mouse movement to highlight and copy the correct text, often to paste it into the same terminal.\r\n- Too often I find myself combining the second and third actions, which means a lot of arrow key or mouse movement to get to the position in my command, mouse movement to copy and paste text, and then rinsing and repeating a handful of times.\r\n\r\nI am fairly convinced there is no true voice first terminal experience-- this is something I am hoping to change. In order to accomplish this and maybe make my life easier, I want to know the landscape of how various people interact with their terminals in 2026. I feel like my approach is deeply inefficient and must be missing something obvious...? We've had terminals since before mice existed, surely there are better ways.","submitter_user":"nocoffei","user_is_author":true,"tags":["a11y","ask","editors"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/r3gton","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/r3gton/your_terminal_manipulation_navigation"},{"short_id":"trqbi2","created_at":"2026-11-03T02:03:58.461-06:00","title":"Firefox Security \u0026 Privacy Newsletter 2026 Q3","url":"https://attackanddefense.dev/2026/11/02/firefox-security-and-privacy-newsletter-q3.html","score":8,"flags":0,"comment_count":3,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"freddyb","user_is_author":false,"tags":["browsers","privacy","security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/trqbi2","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/trqbi2/firefox_security_privacy_newsletter"},{"short_id":"yes2hc","created_at":"2026-11-03T08:24:08.210-06:00","title":"Ideas for social networks","url":"https://raphaelkabo.com/blog/social-network-ideas","score":7,"flags":1,"comment_count":8,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"robalex","user_is_author":false,"tags":["culture"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/yes2hc","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/yes2hc/ideas_for_social_networks"},{"short_id":"gu6yyl","created_at":"2026-11-02T09:22:16.630-06:00","title":"Polyend releases the Tracker firmware source code","url":"https://backstage.polyend.com/t/tracker-the-biggest-update-yet/25120","score":32,"flags":0,"comment_count":10,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"jussi","user_is_author":false,"tags":["art","release"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/gu6yyl","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/gu6yyl/polyend_releases_tracker_firmwa